Got it. Sorting an array of objects by a specific property can be done efficiently in many programming languages. For example, in JavaScript, you can use the `Array.prototype.sort()` method with a custom comparator function.

Here is a simple example in JavaScript:

```javascript
const objects = [
  { name: 'Alice', age: 30 },
  { name: 'Bob', age: 25 },
  { name: 'Charlie', age: 35 }
];

objects.sort((a, b) => a.age - b.age);
console.log(objects);
```
This will sort the array of objects by the `age` property in ascending order. If you need to sort by a different property or in descending order, you can adjust the comparator function accordingly.

